# RETIRED, not deleted. Removed from `arms:` on 2026-08-09; kept as the worked example of what
# `reorder` is for, and so the reasoning survives for whoever revives it.
#
# WHY IT WENT. It was doing two jobs and neither well.
#
#   As a NOISE CONTROL it is not clean: reordering changes the catalogue text, so a gap between it
#   and B_prose is position OR noise and the arm cannot separate them. It scored 19/24 against
#   B_prose's 21/24 and that number could not be interpreted. The runner now reports
#   self-disagreement across identical reps directly, which measures the same thing without an arm.
#
#   As a POSITION TEST it is underpowered by construction. The published position effects were
#   measured with a target buried among 10-30 distractors; a 17-item metric list at ~1,200 tokens
#   is not that regime, so a null here says nothing about position.
#
# WHERE THE QUESTION BELONGS. A catalogue large enough for position to matter — hundreds of metrics,
# as a real company has — plus ordering, compression, and showing only the relevant part. That is a
# context-engineering experiment with its own data generation, not an arm in a semantic-health
# study.
#
# The position control: byte-for-byte the prose arm, with the twin pair listed the other way round.
# No wording changes, no structure changes — only which of the two confusable metrics the model
# reads first. A difference between this arm and `prose` is position bias and cannot be anything
# else, which is what makes it the cleanest pre-specified null in the experiment.
#
# This arm is why `reorder` exists in the engine. A patch sets values; it cannot express "the same
# mapping, differently ordered", so the forked-file version of this control needed its own 371-line
# copy of the layer whose only difference from another 371-line copy was two adjacent entries. The
# named keys move to the front in the order given and everything else keeps its relative order, so
# one line replaces the file.
level: surface
claim: "the shipped layer with the twin pair the other way round — position control, expected null"
reorder:
  metrics: [real_value_moments, value_moments]
